*Message recovery.* Broker-outage recovery remains designed, not tested on AWS: a committed outbox row survives a broker outage, because the relay retries publication to SNS up to ten times (`OutboxRelayService.MAX_ATTEMPTS`). SQS moves a message that fails five receives to its DLQ (AD-04, §3.2.3). Redelivery after a consumer commit is safe, because the consumer records each event in `sys_consumed_message`. Notifications can therefore arrive later, and exhausted relay retries or dead-letter messages need an operator (#src("backend/docs/adr/0007-cross-service-events-sns-sqs.md")[integration decision], #src("backend/blocbuy-infrastructure/src/main/java/com/blocbuy/notification/messaging/SqsNotificationEventConsumer.java")[queue consumer], #src("infra/swe5001/modules/messaging/main.tf")[queue and alarm configuration]).

*V7 poison-message drill.* On 14 November 2026 on AWS, V7 R4 passed five production receives and isolation: the valid transport control produced exactly *1/1/1* consumed marker, notification and intended-recipient notification; poison effects were *0/0* markers/notifications. Only the identified poison was deleted, and quiet queues, a real ALARM transition and its reset were verified. Injection → ALARM was *233.129957 s* and sampled observed DLQ → ALARM *158.232215 s*, both below the unchanged *300 s* limits; the latter is not exact DLQ enqueue latency. The owner's matched email receipt was *10:12 SGT*, minute-only, establishing no exact delivery timestamp or latency. This is notification-transport evidence, not a real-payment test. Sources: `20261114-v7-r4-live/README.md` and its adjacent `acceptance.json` under the §2.2 evidence directory.

That pass used the explicitly approved temporary *10 s application consumer visibility* only: the application image, resources, workers and queue/scaling policies were unchanged, and source-queue visibility remained *60 s*. The original *60 s consumer visibility* was restored; fresh closure passed at *10:31:54 SGT* on 14 November. This is *not a timing pass for the original 60 s consumer*. R1's *515.129 s* injection-to-alarm failure and R2/R3's setup failures before message publication remain failed. The same V7 records retain configuration, restoration and failed-attempt evidence; no release promotion or whole-campaign acceptance follows.

*Identity R1 failed and deferred.* On 13 November 2026 on AWS, START returned *HTTP 200 instead of the expected 500*, and healthy recovery took *217.828 s*, exceeding the unchanged *180 s* gate. Service counts reached zero before the old application shut down, so the probe did not establish application unavailability. Provider payment, signed webhook outage/recovery and duplicate/replay execution were *not run* and remain deferred. The successful local rehearsal in @tbl-availability-failures does not replace these missing AWS gates or pass R1. Source: `20261113-aws-validation/identity-r1.md` under the §2.2 evidence directory.

#block(breakable: false)[*Rebuilding the environment.* On AWS, five clean rebuilds between 17 October and 11 November took 30 min 59 s to 33 min 47 s (@tbl-delivery-timings, §5.3), above the 30 min target (AISS-06). On 14 November, the qualified operator V8 R4 cycle subsequently passed that target with every readiness/health/smoke gate retained, followed by verified destruction of the new runtime and encrypted evidence read-back (§5.3); R1–R3 remain failed. This is one observed pass at the recorded configuration, not a guaranteed recovery time. A rebuild restores service, not data: it is neither a database restore nor uninterrupted service during a zone loss.]
